      "writtenOn": "2020-01-29T10:14:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Oh the reason is stupidly annoying and hard to understand. It has to do with the fact that the NAIs are stateful but some state is deduced from other state and needs to be updated.\n\nSo updateLingerState looks at the state in the nai. It looks up mNum{Request,Background}NetworkRequests which have just been updated, and compares that with mLingering which is a cache. If mLingering is not up to date, it updates it. Then, it returns whether any update was made.\n\nLater in l.6613, any network that was already processed by this loop have already their state updated, so updateLingerState will return false for these networks. That\u0027s why we don\u0027t send duplicate callbacks.\n\nNow, according to the comment in l.6609~6612, some networks not rematched may also have changed lingered state even if it was untouched. E.g. Mobile has score 50, Wifi has score 20 and is unvalidated so it could hope to get to 60. Suddenly Mobile gets to 65, then Wifi can\u0027t ever beat that and should be unlingered and disconnected. But it was not part of the rematch. I am not convinced this is actually possible, because ISTM that any network in this situation would have already its linger state updated by virtue of that change having to result from an earlier pass of rematch. In other words, because the source of truth for linger is ultimately the requests lists stored in the agents and that only changes as part of a rematch, I don\u0027t think it\u0027s actually possible for a non-rematched network to need an update at that point.\nBut. I am not changing that code in this patch, so I think we should simply leave well enough alone.",
